#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193523} :activity/id #uuid "757509db-ea62-5c97-af42-cffc3637196f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048721 :topic/id #uuid "b86bd8ea-6f28-5da3-9761-1ce921d99cf3" :topic/math-academy-id 3945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192681 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192623 :question/id #uuid "1ffdc04e-8224-542d-80f5-99322b693ccf" :question/math-academy-id "e-9836"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7674fd32-6462-5973-9b40-930637b68c85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534047}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 3x3 Covariance Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "2774fb5d-55a7-5428-8581-814bde185950" :step/math-academy-id 36991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193823 :tutorial/content "Recall that the principal components of a standardized observation matrix $X$ are the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$ If \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1,$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nFor example, suppose that the covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is \n\n![](images/e4/e40cd1a0df89ba171b921ab971e8eee25e284265df13b7e031ea32fc28d2c27b.svg)\n\nIt's easy to show that the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=10, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the first principal component of the data, we need the unit eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=10.$ \n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, seeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector \n\n![](images/c7/c7050bbb395b939b1b0559b3e7515c4be4f572aed61090f1a4489e126763240f.svg) \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n![](images/7b/7b0a41deb385aa2471cae701c9f87938085256879a8d834535f8ec0e3f431f27.svg) \n\nTherefore, the $1$st principal component of the data is ![](images/94/9490f2e5105592fa3976ae506cbdf64d70c3372d1cc9295eaa6c6580a4ac75aa.svg)\n\n**Note:** The opposite vector ![](images/e2/e2b366cfb8cb5b708eb5ba443dfc61c05b498d4eb737b375c8ebc86b96e8eea6.svg) also works in the example above." :tutorial/id #uuid "a97acfe0-1488-53e4-bcb3-6876fe2ce042" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6507 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c43bb8da-171f-50ea-8f50-5beb61c94790" :step/math-academy-id 39395 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192401 :question/id #uuid "9f2942ee-4d9f-5436-a021-9e325bfc694b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10450"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40fb56dd-7e99-5cb6-a3f6-f9ddd630c730" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050911}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533999}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 2x2 Covariance Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "2b9d675a-327f-591d-9bc2-fa8d6b6ff934" :step/math-academy-id 39400 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193502}}] :activity/title "Computing Principal Components" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192865}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534141} :activity/id #uuid "6ead580b-4ede-5c00-946f-c5a855bc1388" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048721 :topic/id #uuid "b86bd8ea-6f28-5da3-9761-1ce921d99cf3" :topic/math-academy-id 3945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6ce6b3-e086-5afc-bc5f-380578a38364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533176} {:answer/id #uuid "293116cd-0210-5cb9-b17d-b36dfb030db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533244} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7115fa-f1a5-50b1-8fb7-1bae6211fe0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533438} {:answer/id #uuid "ad077701-5e2a-550b-be10-c54987ab6c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533553} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d6cc6f-6996-5a19-a1b9-8d08475ff929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533176} :answer-field/id #uuid "310815ed-9218-576f-8607-844dee4ec567" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d35740ea-ab16-5dbd-b830-35948c9ebd0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-137739" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $10$ and $5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a3410547-abc5-50e3-b02f-0dc8ed29d6e7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534144}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534144 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18bbaa38-d421-5746-a3c1-8145fc019ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533195} {:answer/id #uuid "2c97f85d-47a1-51b6-a9b9-778680ce8fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533250} {:answer/id #uuid "756ee275-5880-5a22-a4a6-9e1067a5ba8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533418} {:answer/id #uuid "af6affa2-ae7d-5319-81fa-de2ad83b73eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533562} {:answer/id #uuid "ed501507-7e1b-5c82-a8f9-b2410c792d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533195} :answer-field/id #uuid "44831ce7-23e4-5530-a096-5c88232103b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "43345541-f657-59ff-989d-45d4cfc772b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-138506" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1$ and $6.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b50ea736-e70c-587d-b358-26135009e6d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533994 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20807156-9f5d-5d9d-96f8-62147aa1f8b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533215} {:answer/id #uuid "2252d4c1-be79-5250-8ae4-9fec316b035e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533222} {:answer/id #uuid "26c057ba-2bda-5aa2-9455-8957654b4964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533236} {:answer/id #uuid "a7da3ea0-6b29-5fcd-8803-c68581c9ecb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533546} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d2312f-9076-523c-ae85-c86bf3c40aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533570} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5eccd0-37fc-5413-a3f2-96535a173581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39ac48e2-8478-529d-91f9-60e771cc4eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-138510" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $13$ and $3.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cdf898f6-45d1-58e0-a005-fd32969067d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534156}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a0b92ff-1a4f-5a5c-880f-dccb4e0edd45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533245} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f8f5f8-4566-550b-af9b-f5286609b0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533619} {:answer/id #uuid "cff31548-be96-50f5-b36c-0864299f187b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533645} {:answer/id #uuid "d77ccd62-a3e0-5ccb-86f5-b0e37d719ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533665} {:answer/id #uuid "f878ea67-ef32-52af-ab6f-9dbc92982da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533747} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6d3086-6051-52d2-aff8-a787552ec9a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "acbab80b-b003-5343-8102-3dfe5d2664d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-138532" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the third principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $8,$ $5,$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e74a8af4-059f-50bf-807d-00656676699e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097cd6bb-a98e-56ad-9b47-98d16bfbaa02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533155} {:answer/id #uuid "482967d1-9890-55e3-9174-ae742791f383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533306} {:answer/id #uuid "9756f17c-547d-5717-b4bf-797326722482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533511} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb420df-0b0d-5edc-867c-19a182ed465c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533631} {:answer/id #uuid "e1618446-0931-52ac-b916-219dfe148992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533697}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533306} :answer-field/id #uuid "105a3af8-27bf-5111-9830-51162d55cab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a946e21d-5a37-5585-a234-01e6acc34eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-138515" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $2,$ and $9.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ed7a7cf8-ffdb-5b0f-9201-cd08ad8f2b59" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534152}}] :activity/title "Computing Principal Components" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533124}]}
